# Attention as Exact Finite Memory

Begin with the central experiment: a finite query can act as a collision-free address. Select an address and inspect its equality-score row.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The resulting score matrix is the identity, a geometric picture of exact selection.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the equality lemma and proof idea</summary>
For a finite universe $X$, the one-hot vector $e_x$ has a single nonzero coordinate. Hence $e_x\cdot e_a=1$ when $x=a$ and $0$ otherwise. Multiplying a stored value by this score makes one head fire and all others vanish.
</details>

## Compiling a finite function

The first algorithm turns any finite vector-valued table into one lookup head per input. Its output is exact rather than approximate.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

The complete data flow is address, equality comparison, value gating, and summation.

{{visualization:2}}

## From individual addresses to sequences

A whole fixed-length sequence over a finite alphabet can be treated as one address. Toggle this binary sequence to explore an eight-head example.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the sequence universality theorem</summary>
If an alphabet has $v$ symbols and input length is $n$, there are $v^n$ input sequences. For any map from these sequences to an $m\times r$ real array, assign one head to each sequence and store its target array. One-hot attention selects exactly the matching array at every output coordinate.
</details>

Exact universality has an exponential resource price. The plot below displays $2^n$ heads for binary sequences.

{{visualization:1}}

For background on practical attention architectures, see [The Illustrated Transformer](https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/) and the [original transformer paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762). The theorem here concerns unnormalized linear attention and finite lookup, not ordinary finite-temperature softmax.

## Algebraic simplification

Attention's score is bilinear, so mixtures of queries and keys expand by superposition.

{{algorithm:2}}

Position vectors add under composition. Learned coordinatewise affine stages also fuse exactly.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the affine composition law</summary>
For $A_{s,b}(x)=s\odot x+b$, direct expansion gives $A_{s_2,b_2}(A_{s_1,b_1}(x))=A_{s_2\odot s_1,\,s_2\odot b_1+b_2}(x)$. An additive position $p$ followed by this affine stage contributes $s\odot p+b$ to the effective bias.
</details>

## Where the boundary lies

The construction proves exact representation on a finite domain with one head per possible input. Continuous approximation, softmax error bounds, full data-dependent layer normalization, and efficient compression require additional arguments. Those boundaries suggest the next research program rather than weakening the exact finite result.
